<node id="1225440" title="Re^6: webperl: fetching data over the web" created="2018-11-08 13:06:58" updated="2018-11-08 13:06:58" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;p&gt;Thank you, with that HTML file I can reproduce it too. (BTW, &lt;c&gt;req.open('GET', 'http://localhost:5000/emperl.js', false);&lt;/c&gt; should probably be &lt;c&gt;req.open('GET', url, false);&lt;/c&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;c&gt;emperl.js&lt;/c&gt; is pretty huge, so I tried changing it to &lt;c&gt;webperl.js&lt;/c&gt;, and although it exhibited the same behavior of seeming to block the webpage, after a little while it got unstuck and displayed the output. If you do &lt;c&gt;print length(js(...))&lt;/c&gt; instead of &lt;c&gt;print js()&lt;/c&gt;, you get a result much quicker. The same is true if you remove the &lt;c&gt;&lt;textarea&gt;&lt;/c&gt; from the HTML page, which causes the output to go to the JS console. There is obviously something very inefficient going on with the &lt;c&gt;&lt;textarea&gt;&lt;/c&gt;, I'll take a guess that it's [https://github.com/haukex/webperl/blob/f7b5822176ce65f67d906c9d1801e614c7abacce/web/webperl.js#L177|this line]: &lt;c&gt;ta.value = ta.value + str;&lt;/c&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I didn't put a lot of effort into optimizing the &lt;c&gt;STDOUT/ERR&lt;/c&gt; routines because in the WebPerl applications I was imagining, there wouldn't be any console output, and everything would get handled through the UI. But in the case of this textarea, there's certainly a lot of room for improvement. (Patches welcome &lt;c&gt;:-)&lt;/c&gt;&amp;nbsp;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The endless loop of initializing/ended is also interesting, something I haven't seen yet, could you tell me the steps to reproduce that?&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
